<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trois-Rivières</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trois-Rivières is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, where the Saint-Maurice river meets the St Lawrence, halfway between Quebec City and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. Founded in 1634 by Laviolette on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a>'s orders, it was the second permanent French settlement on the St Lawrence and a fur-trading post. It later became an industrial centre known for pulp and paper.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trois-rivieres.jpg" alt="Trois-Rivières" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quintin Soloviev · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trois-Rivi%C3%A8res,_Quebec_(cropped)2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Trois-Rivières lies on the north shore of the Saint Lawrence River at the mouth of the Saint-Maurice River, in the Mauricie region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. The name means three rivers and refers to the three channels into which the Saint-Maurice is divided at its mouth by two islands. François Gravé Du Pont, a geographer under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a>, first used the name in 1599, when sailing upriver he took the channels for three separate tributaries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For thousands of years the area was frequented by Indigenous peoples. The Algonquin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a> used it as a summer stopping place to fish, hunt and gather roots and nuts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a> described the site in 1535 and planted a cross on Saint-Quentin Island. In 1603 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> recommended a permanent settlement, and the village was formed on 4 July 1634 under its first governor, Sieur de Laviolette. It played an important part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> with First Nations peoples and became the seat of a regional government in 1665. Ursuline nuns who arrived in 1697 founded its first school.</p><p>French rule ended in 1760, when the town was taken in the British conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. On 8 June 1776 the Battle of Trois-Rivières was fought here during the American invasion of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. Its first foundry opened in 1738 and supplied iron, much of it shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for the navy, for about 150 years. A fire in 1908 destroyed most of the city, including many buildings from the French colonial years; the Ursuline Monastery and the De Tonnancour Manor survived.</p><p>From the late 1920s to the early 1960s the city was known as the pulp and paper capital of the world. Industry declined later, and in the 1960s the city began to diversify its economy. The Old City was declared an historic sector in 1964 and the Université du Québec à Trois-Rivières was founded in 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Trois-Rivières is the economic and cultural hub of the Mauricie region, roughly halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> and Quebec City, and French is the mother tongue of the great majority of its people. Its landmarks include the Forges du Saint-Maurice, a foundry dating from the 1730s, and the Notre-Dame-du-Cap Basilica. It has a major ice-free port on the Saint Lawrence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trois-Rivières” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Trois-Rivières</b> was founded in 1634 and Ville-Marie, later Montreal, in 1642 by Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve and Jeanne Mance as a religious mission.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
